Welcome to tmux!

tmux is a terminal multiplexer: it enables a number of terminals to be created,
accessed, and controlled from a single screen. tmux may be detached from a
screen and continue running in the background, then later reattached.

This release runs on OpenBSD, FreeBSD, NetBSD, Linux, macOS and Solaris.

* Fork experiment: interactive PTY handoff

This fork adds explicit park-pane, adopt-pane, handoff-status and
handoff-cancel commands. A keeper retains the original PTY master and a bounded
1 MiB output FIFO while the old server exits. Adoption transfers that descriptor
to an empty pane in a new server; it does not restart the application.

Use only private test servers until the surrounding application's lifecycle
supports migration. For an 80x24 pane, with absolute binary and private paths:

	$ mkdir -m 700 /path/to/private/handoff
	$ tmux -S /path/to/private/old park-pane -t %0 /path/to/private/handoff
	$ tmux -S /path/to/private/old kill-server
	$ tmux -S /path/to/private/new new-session -d -s resumed -x 80 -y 24
	$ tmux -S /path/to/private/new new-window -k -t resumed:0 -P -F '#{pane_id}' ''
	$ tmux -S /path/to/private/new adopt-pane -t %0 /path/to/private/handoff

Use the pane ID printed by new-window, not the illustrative %0, and preserve
the original pane dimensions. new-session with an empty command is not an empty
adoption target. handoff-status reports keeper state; handoff-cancel closes a
parked PTY and may terminate its application. It is not a harmless cleanup.

Parking requires a supported parser checkpoint. Partial UTF-8/escape sequences,
synchronized updates, nondefault attributes/charsets/hyperlinks, custom tabs,
palette or scroll margins, pending replies and active pane modes/queues are
refused. The active viewport and supported cursor/parser state are restored;
normal-screen history beneath an alternate screen is not restored. Inherited
TMUX/TMUX_PANE variables remain stale, so this is not transparent Vibeterm
process migration. An adopted process is not the new server's child: EOF marks
it dead with unknown (empty) exit status.

Commit acknowledgement ambiguity leaves the descriptor suspended, never with
two active readers; repeat the same command to resolve keeper ownership. The
private directory must belong to the current user with mode 0700. This is a
same-user mechanism, not protection from another process owned by that user.
PID-only server restart is tested on macOS. Supervisor control-group shutdown
requires separate Linux cgroup validation and is not implied by those tests.

The same PID-only regression also passes on Linux with systemd disabled. The
keeper is forked without exec and retains the server's inherited address space;
the 1 MiB FIFO bound is not a bound on total keeper memory. After adoption it
retains the receipt endpoint until handoff-cancel, which then removes only the
receipt and does not close the already transferred PTY. Unresolved handoffs
refuse pane/window respawn until ownership is positively recovered.

Run the isolated end-to-end regression from this checkout:

	$ python3 -B -m regress.handoff ./tmux

It checks actual server replacement with unchanged application PID/TTY and job
control, viewport/input continuity, lost commit acknowledgements, fragment
boundaries, cancellation, EOF and exact 2 MiB output parity. It never connects
to the default server.

* Dependencies

tmux depends on libevent 2.x, available from:

	https://github.com/libevent/libevent/releases/latest

It also depends on ncurses, available from:

	https://invisible-mirror.net/archives/ncurses/

To build tmux, a C compiler (for example gcc or clang), make, pkg-config and a
suitable yacc (yacc or bison) are needed.

* Installation

To build and install tmux from a release tarball, use:

	$ ./configure && make
	$ sudo make install

tmux can use the utempter library to update utmp(5), if it is installed - run
configure with --enable-utempter to enable this.

To get and build the latest from version control - note that this requires
autoconf, automake and pkg-config:

	$ git clone https://github.com/tmux/tmux.git
	$ cd tmux
	$ sh autogen.sh
	$ ./configure && make
	$ sudo make install

* Contributing

Bug reports, feature suggestions and especially code contributions are most
welcome. Please send by email to:

	tmux-users@googlegroups.com

Or open a GitHub issue or pull request.

* Documentation

For documentation on using tmux, see the tmux.1 manpage. View it from the
source tree with:

	$ nroff -mdoc tmux.1|less

A small example configuration is in example_tmux.conf.

Other documentation is available in the wiki:

	https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki

Also see the tmux FAQ at:

	https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/FAQ

A bash(1) completion file is at:

	https://github.com/scop/bash-completion/blob/main/completions/tmux

For debugging, run tmux with -v and -vv to generate server and client log files
in the current directory.

* Support

The tmux mailing list for general discussion and bug reports is:

	https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/tmux-users

Subscribe by sending an email to:

	tmux-users+subscribe@googlegroups.com

